## 2. Namespaces (`ns` form)

### 2.1 One namespace per file, one file per namespace — 🟠 HIGH
- Never `in-ns` across files. Never declare multiple `ns` forms.

### 2.2 No single-segment namespaces — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
(ns example.ns)         ; ✅
(ns example)            ; ❌
```

### 2.3 Max 5 namespace segments — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Avoid overly long, deeply nested namespaces.

### 2.4 Comprehensive `ns` form order — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Order: `:refer-clojure`, then `:require`, then `:import`.

### 2.5 One require/import per line; sorted alphabetically — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
(ns examples.ns
  (:require
   [baz.core :as baz]
   [clojure.java.shell :as sh]
   [clojure.set :as set]
   [clojure.string :as str :refer [blank?]]
   [foo.bar :as foo]))
```
- Sorted requires improve diff quality and prevent duplicates.

### 2.6 Prefer `:require :as` — 🟠 HIGH
- Preference order: `:require :as` > `:require :refer [...]` > `:require :refer :all` > `:use` (deprecated — never use in new code).

### 2.7 Idiomatic namespace aliases — 🟡 MEDIUM

Use these aliases when requiring common namespaces. Inconsistency across a codebase is a finding.

| Namespace | Idiomatic alias |
|---|---|
| `clojure.string` | `str` |
| `clojure.set` | `set` |
| `clojure.walk` | `walk` |
| `clojure.zip` | `zip` |
| `clojure.edn` | `edn` |
| `clojure.java.io` | `io` |
| `clojure.java.shell` | `sh` |
| `clojure.math` | `math` |
| `clojure.datafy` | `datafy` |
| `clojure.pprint` | `pp` |
| `clojure.spec.alpha` | `s` |
| `clojure.core.async` | `async` |
| `clojure.tools.logging` | `log` |
| `clojure.tools.cli` | `cli` |
| `clojure.data.csv` | `csv` |
| `clojure.data.xml` | `xml` |
| `cheshire.core` | `json` |
| `clj-yaml.core` | `yaml` |
| `clj-http.client` | `http` |
| `next.jdbc` | `jdbc` |
| `java-time` | `time` |
| `hugsql.core` | `sql` |

### 2.8 Alias derivation recipe for non-idiomatic namespaces — 🔵 LOW
- Drop leading segments; keep enough trailing segments to stay unique. Dotted aliases are fine.
- Eliminate redundant words (`core`, `clj`).
```clojure
[clj-time.core     :as time]
[clj-time.format   :as time.format]
```

