## 5. Core Idioms

### 5.1 `when` vs `if` — 🟠 HIGH
- Use `when` instead of `if` with only a truthy branch.
```clojure
(when pred (foo) (bar))         ; ✅
(if pred (do (foo) (bar)))      ; ❌
```

### 5.2 `if-let` / `when-let` — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
(if-let [r (foo x)]  (use r) (fallback))   ; ✅
(when-let [r (foo x)] (use r) (more r))    ; ✅
```

### 5.3 `if-not` / `when-not` — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
(if-not pred (foo))             ; ✅
(if (not pred) (foo))           ; ❌

(when-not pred (foo) (bar))     ; ✅
(when (not pred) ...)           ; ❌
(if-not pred (do (foo) (bar)))  ; ❌  use when-not
```

### 5.4 `not=` — 🔵 LOW
```clojure
(not= a b)                      ; ✅
(not (= a b))                   ; ❌
```

### 5.5 `inc` / `dec` / `pos?` / `neg?` / `zero?` — 🔵 LOW
```clojure
(inc x) (dec x)                 ; ✅
(+ x 1) (- x 1)                 ; ❌

(pos? x) (neg? x) (zero? x)     ; ✅
(> x 0) (< x 0) (= x 0)         ; ❌
```

### 5.6 Flexible comparison chaining — 🔵 LOW
```clojure
(< 5 x 10)                      ; ✅
(and (> x 5) (< x 10))          ; ❌
```

### 5.7 Nil punning for empty sequence check — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
(when (seq s) ...)              ; ✅
(when-not (empty? s) ...)       ; ❌
```

### 5.8 `vec` over `(into [] ...)` — 🔵 LOW
```clojure
(vec some-seq)                  ; ✅
(into [] some-seq)              ; ❌
```

### 5.9 `boolean` for truthy coercion — 🔵 LOW
```clojure
(boolean x)                     ; ✅
(if x true false)               ; ❌
```

### 5.10 Prefer higher-order fns over `loop`/`recur` — 🟠 HIGH
- Replace manual loops with `map`, `filter`, `reduce`, `mapcat`, `keep`, `some`, `reductions`.
- `loop`/`recur` acceptable only for custom control flow or stack-safe recursion.

### 5.11 Function literals `#(...)` — 🔵 LOW
- Single param → `%` (not `%1`).
- Multiple params → `%1`, `%2`.
- Don't use `#()` for bodies with multiple forms — use `(fn [x] ...)`.
- Don't wrap an existing fn unnecessarily: `(filter even? coll)` not `(filter #(even? %) coll)`.

### 5.12 Prefer anonymous fns over `complement`/`comp`/`partial` — 🔵 LOW
- For most cases, `#(not (pred? %))` is clearer than `(complement pred?)`.
- Exception: `comp` is idiomatic for **transducer chains**.

### 5.13 Threading macros — 🟠 HIGH

**Use `->` / `->>` for linear pipelines of transformations.**

- `->` threads value as **first** arg (use for `assoc`, `update`, `get`, `conj`).
- `->>` threads value as **last** arg (use for `map`, `filter`, `reduce`, `remove`).
- Mixing them wrong = 🟠 HIGH finding.

**When to use:**
- Series of pure transformations
- Output of each step feeds the next
- Pipeline reads top-to-bottom

**When to avoid:**
- Branching / conditional logic → use `cond->`, `cond->>`, `let`, `cond`
- Single call or 2-step pipeline where threading adds ceremony

**Formatting:**
- No parens for zero-arg fn calls: `(-> x fizz :foo first frob)` ✅
- Parens required when passing args: `(-> x (fizz a b) :foo)` ✅
- Vertically align threaded steps:
```clojure
(->> (range)
     (filter even?)
     (take 5))                  ; ✅
```

### 5.14 `cond` idioms — 🟠 HIGH
- `:else` as final catch-all (not `true`, not `:default`):
```clojure
(cond
  (neg? n) "negative"
  (pos? n) "positive"
  :else    "zero")              ; ✅
```
- **`condp`** > `cond` when predicate + expr don't change:
```clojure
(condp = x
  10 :ten
  20 :twenty
  :dunno)                       ; ✅
```
- **`case`** > `condp` when test values are compile-time constants:
```clojure
(case x
  10 :ten
  20 :twenty
  :dunno)                       ; ✅
```

### 5.15 Short forms in `cond` — 🟡 MEDIUM
- If all branches fit one line, align them. If they don't fit, separate pairs with blank lines or comments for visual grouping.

### 5.16 Set as predicate — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
(remove #{1} coll)                                       ; ✅
(filter #{\a \e \i \o \u} "string")                      ; ✅
(remove #(= % 1) coll)                                   ; ❌
```

### 5.17 Keywords / maps / collections as functions — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
(:name m)                   ; ✅  keyword as fn on map
(get m :name)               ; ❌  verbose
(m :name)                   ; ❌  NPE risk if m is nil

((juxt :a :b) {:a 1 :b 2})  ; ✅  keywords as fns
```

### 5.18 `list*` over nested `cons` — 🔵 LOW
```clojure
(list* 1 2 3 [4 5])                 ; ✅
(cons 1 (cons 2 (cons 3 [4 5])))    ; ❌
```

### 5.19 Sugared Java interop — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
(java.util.ArrayList. 100)   ; ✅ object creation
(new java.util.ArrayList 100); ❌

(Math/pow 2 10)              ; ✅ static method
(.substring "hello" 1 3)     ; ✅ instance method
Integer/MAX_VALUE            ; ✅ static field
(.someField obj)             ; ✅ instance field
```

### 5.20 Compact metadata for boolean flags — 🔵 LOW
```clojure
(def ^:private a 5)                   ; ✅
(def ^{:private true} a 5)            ; ❌ verbose
```

### 5.21 Shadowing / rebinding — 🟠 HIGH
- Don't shadow `clojure.core` names with local bindings (e.g., don't name params `map`, `list`, `str`, `key`).
- Don't `def` inside a function body.
- Use `alter-var-root` (not `def`) to change a var's value at runtime.

### 5.22 Forward references — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Avoid. If needed, use `declare`.

