## 6. Data Structures

### 6.1 Avoid lists for generic storage — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Use vectors, maps, or sets unless list semantics are specifically required.

### 6.2 Keywords as map keys — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
{:name "Bruce" :age 30}          ; ✅
{"name" "Bruce" "age" 30}        ; ❌
```

### 6.3 Literal collection syntax — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
[1 2 3]                          ; ✅
#{1 2 3}                         ; ✅ (only for compile-time constants)
(vector 1 2 3)                   ; ❌
(hash-set 1 2 3)                 ; ❌
#{(f) (g)}                       ; ❌ runtime error if values collide
(hash-set (f) (g))               ; ✅ runtime-computed set
```

### 6.4 Avoid index-based access — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Use destructuring, `first`/`second`/`nth`, or keyword access.

### 6.5 Avoid transients, Java collections, Java arrays — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Transients: only for perf-critical hot paths.
- Java collections/arrays: only for interop or primitive-heavy perf code.

---

## 7. Records, Types, Protocols

### 7.1 Use factory fns, not interop constructors — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
(->Foo 1 2)                   ; ✅
(map->Foo {:a 1 :b 2})        ; ✅ (records only)
(Foo. 1 2)                    ; ❌
```

### 7.2 Custom constructors — 🟡 MEDIUM
- When you need validation, wrap in a named fn (e.g., `make-customer`). Don't override the auto-generated `->Foo` / `map->Foo`.

---

## 8. Mutation

### 8.1 Refs
- Wrap I/O in `io!` macro so accidental transactional use throws.
- Prefer `alter` over `ref-set` — 🟡 MEDIUM.
- Keep transactions small; don't mix short/long running transactions on same ref.

### 8.2 Agents
- `send` — CPU-bound work, never blocks.
- `send-off` — blocking / I/O / sleeping work.
- Using the wrong one = 🟠 HIGH.

### 8.3 Atoms
- Prefer `swap!` over `reset!` when reading the current value matters — 🟡 MEDIUM.
- Don't update atoms inside STM transactions — 🔴 CRITICAL (breaks transactional semantics).

---

## 9. Math & Strings

### 9.1 Prefer `clojure.math` over Java interop — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
(math/pow 2 5)                ; ✅
(Math/pow 2 5)                ; ok-ish (pre-1.11 acceptable)
```

### 9.2 Prefer `clojure.string` over Java interop — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
(str/upper-case s)            ; ✅
(.toUpperCase s)              ; ❌
```

---

## 10. Exceptions

### 10.1 Reuse existing exception types — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Throw `IllegalArgumentException`, `IllegalStateException`, `UnsupportedOperationException`, `IOException` rather than inventing new ones.

### 10.2 `with-open` over `try/finally` for closeables — 🟠 HIGH

---

## 11. Macros

### 11.1 Don't write a macro when a fn will do — 🟠 HIGH
### 11.2 Write macro usage example first, then the macro — 🔵 LOW
### 11.3 Break complicated macros into helper fns — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Macro = syntactic sugar. The core logic should be a plain function.
### 11.4 Prefer syntax-quoted forms (`` `form ``) over manual list construction — 🟡 MEDIUM

---

## 12. `let` Bindings

### 12.1 When to use `let` — ✅
- Value used more than once
- Name improves readability
- Avoids recomputation

### 12.2 When to avoid `let` — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Single-use throwaway values
- When threading or `comp` expresses intent more directly
```clojure
;; ❌ redundant let
(let [doubled (map #(* 2 %) xs)
      big     (filter #(> % 10) doubled)]
  big)

;; ✅ threading
(->> xs (map #(* 2 %)) (filter #(> % 10)))
```

---

## 13. `map` / `filter` / `reduce`

Replace manual `loop`/`recur` with sequence ops whenever possible — 🟠 HIGH.

```clojure
(map inc xs)                          ; ✅
(filter even? xs)                     ; ✅
(reduce + xs)                         ; ✅
(reduce (fn [acc x] (assoc acc (:id x) x)) {} records)  ; ✅
```

---

## 14. Metadata Conventions

Recommended metadata keys for public APIs:

- `:added "0.5"` — version introduced (omit patch for SemVer)
- `:changed "0.6"` — version of last behavior change
- `:deprecated "0.8"` or `:deprecated true`
- `:superseded-by "new-fn"` or `"other.ns/new-fn"`
- `:see-also ["related-fn" "other.ns/another-fn"]`
- `:no-doc true` — hide from generated API docs
- `:style/indent N` — indentation hint for CIDER/tooling

---

## 15. Comments

### 15.1 Structure — 🔵 LOW
- `;;;;` — heading / section separator
- `;;;` — top-level comment (outside any def)
- `;;` — fragment/line comment, above the code it describes
- `;` — margin/inline comment (end of line)

### 15.2 Content rules — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Always at least one space after the semicolons.
- Complete sentences, capitalized, punctuated.
- No superfluous comments: `(inc x) ; increments x by 1` is noise.
- Keep comments up-to-date — outdated comments are worse than none.

### 15.3 Commenting out code — 🔵 LOW
- Prefer `#_(form)` reader macro over `;; (form)`:
```clojure
(+ foo #_(bar x) delta)              ; ✅
```

### 15.4 Annotations — 🔵 LOW
Place above the code, include a colon + space + note. Sign with initials + date.

| Tag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `TODO:` | missing feature to add |
| `FIXME:` | broken code needing fix |
| `OPTIMIZE:` | slow / inefficient |
| `HACK:` | questionable practice, refactor later |
| `REVIEW:` | confirm this works as intended |

```clojure
(defn handle []
  ;; FIXME: Crashes intermittently since v1.2.3. May be related
  ;;        to BarBazUtil upgrade. (uj 26-04-21)
  (baz))
```

---

## 16. Documentation (Docstrings)

### 16.1 Prefer docstrings over `:doc` metadata — 🟠 HIGH
```clojure
(defn foo "Does a thing." [] ...)             ; ✅
(defn foo ^{:doc "Does a thing."} [] ...)     ; ❌
```

### 16.2 First sentence = concise summary — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Tooling (CIDER, Cursive, cljdoc) shows only the first line in tooltips.
```clojure
(defn frobnitz
  "Computes the frobnitz of `coll`.
  Additional paragraphs go after the summary line and describe
  details, options, edge cases."
  [coll] ...)
```

### 16.3 Leverage Markdown — 🔵 LOW
- cljdoc renders Markdown: tables, code fences, links.

### 16.4 Backtick positional args — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
(defn watsitz
  "Converts `frob` to a znoot. If `frob` is negative the znoot is angry."
  [frob] ...)                                 ; ✅
```

### 16.5 Reference other vars with `` ` `` or `[[..]]` — 🔵 LOW
- `` `clojure.core/identity` `` for a name reference
- `[[kangaroo]]` for a link (cljdoc / Codox)

### 16.6 Indentation & whitespace — 🔵 LOW
- Indent continuation lines by 2 spaces.
- No leading/trailing whitespace inside the docstring.

### 16.7 Protocol method docstrings — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Place **after** the arg vector (different from `defn`):
```clojure
(defprotocol MyProtocol
  "Protocol docstring."
  (foo [this x]
    "Method docstring after the argv."))
```

---

## 17. Testing

### 17.1 Directory structure — 🟡 MEDIUM
- Tests live in `test/yourproject/...`, mirroring `src/yourproject/...`.

### 17.2 Namespace naming — 🟠 HIGH
- `yourproject.something-test` → file `test/yourproject/something_test.clj` (or `.cljc` / `.cljs`).

### 17.3 Test fn naming — 🟠 HIGH
- `deftest something-test` — singular `-test` suffix.
```clojure
(deftest something-test ...)         ; ✅
(deftest something-tests ...)        ; ❌ plural
(deftest test-something ...)         ; ❌ prefix
(deftest something ...)              ; ❌ missing suffix
```

### 17.4 Structure: `deftest` + `testing` — 🟡 MEDIUM
- One `deftest` per behavior group.
- `testing` blocks describe each scenario in plain English.

```clojure
(deftest valid-user?-test
  (testing "returns true for active + verified"
    (is (true? (valid-user? {:status :active :email-verified? true}))))
  (testing "returns false when email unverified"
    (is (false? (valid-user? {:status :active :email-verified? false})))))
```

### 17.5 Use `constantly` for fixed-return mocks — 🟡 MEDIUM
```clojure
(deftest test-some-function
  (testing "returns expected response"
    (bond/with-local-stub [[ma/create-user (constantly {:id "hsft_user_1"})]]
      (is (= {:status :ok}
             (hwi/some-function {:did "did"
                                 :identities [{:uid "hsft_user_1"}
                                              {:email "user12@gmail.com"}]}))))))
```
- `constantly` returns a fn of any arity that always returns the given value — ideal for stubs where arg count/value doesn't matter.

### 17.6 Use predicate assertions — 🟠 HIGH
- `nil?`, `true?`, `false?` are more accurate than `(= nil ...)` / `(= true ...)` (truthy ≠ `true`).
```clojure
(is (true?  (do-task req)))              ; ✅
(is (false? (do-task req)))              ; ✅
(is (nil?   (do-task req)))              ; ✅

(is (= true  (do-task req)))             ; ❌
(is (= false (do-task req)))             ; ❌
(is (= nil   (do-task req)))             ; ❌
```

